1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,640
In summer 1940 the Luftwaffe had

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:08,010
more than three times as many
aircraft as RAF Fighter Command.

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,770
It was to win control
of the sky over England

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,850
so that the Wehrmacht
could cross the Channel.

5
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:16,990
But four months later the
invasion had been called off,

6
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:21,850
and for the first time in this war
Germany had failed to reach its goal.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,160
After the victory over
France, Hitler expected

8
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:28,240
that Britain would give in.

9
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:33,040
On 19 July 1940 he offered
peace in the Reichstag

10
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:36,510
and called it an appeal to reason.

11
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:40,610
On the same day he promoted
twelve generals to field marshal,

12
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,070
and Hermann Göring even
became Reichsmarschall.

13
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:46,730
Three days later London said no.

14
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,640
Prime Minister Winston Churchill
had already announced in June

15
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:53,980
that Britain would fight
on, alone if necessary.

16
00:00:55,530 --> 00:01:01,120
On 16 July Hitler had already
signed Directive No. 16.

17
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:04,110
It ordered preparations
for a landing in England,

18
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:06,940
under the code name Operation Sea Lion.

19
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:10,140
But the Wehrmacht had no landing fleet.

20
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:14,580
So the navy brought more than
2,000 river barges from Germany,

21
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:17,800
Belgium and the Netherlands
to the Channel coast.

22
00:01:18,010 --> 00:01:22,480
Many had no engine of their own
and had to be towed by tugs.

23
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:27,690
The Kriegsmarine had lost
many ships off Norway

24
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:30,820
and was far weaker than the British fleet.

25
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:32,750
A landing was only possible

26
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:35,900
if the Royal Air Force
could no longer attack it.

27
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:39,940
So the Luftwaffe was to destroy
the British fighters first.

28
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:43,620
Göring promised it would
take only a few weeks.

29
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,320
Three air fleets were ready for the task.

30
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:51,250
Luftflotte 2 under Albert Kesselring
was in Belgium and northern France,

31
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:56,240
Luftflotte 3 under Hugo
Sperrle further west in France,

32
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,640
Luftflotte 5 in Norway and Denmark.

33
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:04,520
Together they had about 2,500
aircraft ready for action:

34
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:11,960
Heinkel He 111, Dornier Do
17 and Junkers Ju 88 bombers,

35
00:02:12,410 --> 00:02:15,000
Ju 87 dive bombers,

36
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:20,800
and Messerschmitt Bf
109 and Bf 110 fighters.

37
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:27,490
On the other side stood Fighter Command
under Air Chief Marshal Hugh Dowding,

38
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,450
with just over 600 serviceable fighters,

39
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:32,780
mainly Hurricanes and Spitfires.

40
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:36,400
Before the war Dowding had built a system

41
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:38,860
that existed nowhere else.

42
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:42,880
Along the coast stood the radar
stations of the Chain Home network,

43
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:46,380
which could detect approaching formations
while they were still over France.

44
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:50,830
Inland, thousands of observers of
the Royal Observer Corps reported

45
00:02:50,850 --> 00:02:53,670
every aircraft they saw or heard.

46
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:59,200
All reports came together at
Bentley Priory near London.

47
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,660
There they were checked and
passed on to the control rooms.

48
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:06,730
On large map tables, women of
the Women's Auxiliary Air Force

49
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:08,670
moved markers across the map,

50
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:12,620
and the officers on the gallery
could see where the enemy was flying.

51
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:15,780
That way they could send their squadrons
exactly where they were needed.

52
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:20,110
The British fighters did not have to
wait on patrol. They only took off

53
00:03:20,130 --> 00:03:21,460
once the attack came.

54
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:26,480
The fighting began in July
over the English Channel.

55
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:30,930
German Stukas and bombers
attacked British convoys and ports

56
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:33,700
to lure the RAF fighters into battle.

57
00:03:33,730 --> 00:03:37,360
German pilots called these weeks
the Kanalkampf, the Channel battle.

58
00:03:37,610 --> 00:03:42,080
Both sides lost aircraft,
but nothing was decided.

59
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:45,630
On 1 August Hitler ordered
in Directive No. 17

60
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:49,210
that the RAF be defeated
as quickly as possible.

61
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:54,500
On 12 August German aircraft
attacked several radar stations.

62
00:03:54,610 --> 00:03:58,530
The station at Ventnor on the Isle
of Wight was out of action for days,

63
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:01,320
the others were transmitting
again within hours.

64
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:06,320
The German command underestimated
radar and soon stopped attacking it.

65
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:13,200
On 13 August came Adlertag, Eagle
Day, the real start of the offensive.

66
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:16,990
On 15 August the Luftwaffe
flew more sorties

67
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:19,090
than on any other day of the battle.

68
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:23,530
Luftflotte 5 sent bombers from
Norway to northern England,

69
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:25,800
expecting little resistance there.

70
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:30,650
But radar had picked them up early,
and they lost so many aircraft

71
00:04:30,670 --> 00:04:32,620
that they never attacked by day again.

72
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:37,460
In the Luftwaffe this day later
became known as Black Thursday.

73
00:04:37,530 --> 00:04:41,390
On 18 August the Stukas
suffered such heavy losses

74
00:04:41,410 --> 00:04:43,280
that they were withdrawn from the battle.

75
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:47,730
The slow dive bombers could hardly
be protected against modern fighters.

76
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:52,630
The twin-engined Bf 110 was also
inferior to the Hurricanes and Spitfires

77
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:54,380
in a dogfight.

78
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:59,240
Soon even these heavy fighters
needed a Bf 109 escort.

79
00:05:00,790 --> 00:05:04,820
The Bf 109 was a match
for the British fighters,

80
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:06,880
but it had another problem.

81
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:11,420
Over London its fuel lasted for
only about ten minutes of combat.

82
00:05:11,590 --> 00:05:14,830
Göring demanded that the fighters
stay close to the bombers.

83
00:05:14,850 --> 00:05:17,970
So the pilots lost their
greatest advantage,

84
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:20,620
height and surprise.

85
00:05:20,690 --> 00:05:24,660
When Göring asked the fighter
pilot Adolf Galland what he needed,

86
00:05:24,730 --> 00:05:28,120
he is said to have answered:
a squadron of Spitfires.

87
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:33,330
At the end of August the Luftwaffe
targeted the fighter airfields

88
00:05:33,350 --> 00:05:37,900
in south-east England, such as
Biggin Hill, Kenley and Manston.

89
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:40,640
Fighter Command was now in danger.

90
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:43,690
British factories could
replace aircraft quickly,

91
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:47,900
in summer 1940 they built more
fighters than the German ones.

92
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:50,320
But experienced pilots were scarce.

93
00:05:50,410 --> 00:05:54,820
Among them were Poles, Czechs,
Canadians and New Zealanders.

94
00:05:55,010 --> 00:05:58,390
The Polish 303 Squadron
claimed more victories

95
00:05:58,410 --> 00:06:01,140
than any other squadron
in Fighter Command.

96
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:07,060
On 20 August Churchill told the House
of Commons about the fighter pilots:

97
00:06:07,230 --> 00:06:09,930
Never in the field of human conflict

98
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:14,150
was so much owed by so many to so few.

99
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:17,350
Since then the roughly 3,000 men

100
00:06:17,370 --> 00:06:19,730
who flew for Fighter Command in the battle

101
00:06:19,750 --> 00:06:23,230
have been known in Britain as "The Few".

102
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:28,260
On the night of 24 to 25 August
German bombers, apparently by mistake,

103
00:06:28,310 --> 00:06:29,440
dropped bombs on London.

104
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:32,820
The next night Churchill
had Berlin bombed.

105
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:36,040
The damage was slight,
but Göring had promised

106
00:06:36,110 --> 00:06:39,280
that no enemy bomber would
ever reach the Reich.

107
00:06:39,430 --> 00:06:42,610
On 4 September in the Berlin
Sportpalast Hitler threatened

108
00:06:42,630 --> 00:06:45,560
to wipe out British cities.

109
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:50,650
On 7 September about 350 bombers,

110
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:53,660
escorted by more than 600
fighters, flew to London.

111
00:06:53,910 --> 00:06:57,020
Göring watched them leave
from the French coast.

112
00:06:57,290 --> 00:07:01,600
The bombs hit mainly the docks and the
residential districts of the East End.

113
00:07:01,750 --> 00:07:06,380
The fires could be seen far into the
night, and more than 400 people died.

114
00:07:06,470 --> 00:07:10,660
From then on London was
bombed for 57 nights in a row.

115
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:15,380
But for the exhausted fighter pilots
the change meant a breathing space,

116
00:07:15,510 --> 00:07:18,520
because their airfields were
hardly attacked any more.

117
00:07:20,070 --> 00:07:25,260
On 15 September the Luftwaffe attacked
London twice with large formations.

118
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:29,760
That day Churchill was in the operations
room of No. 11 Group at Uxbridge.

119
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:34,220
He asked Air Vice Marshal Keith
Park what reserves were left.

120
00:07:34,290 --> 00:07:36,910
Park replied that there were none.

121
00:07:36,930 --> 00:07:40,970
Even so, the formations were
intercepted and lost about 60 aircraft.

122
00:07:40,990 --> 00:07:46,320
To this day the British mark this
date as Battle of Britain Day.

123
00:07:46,370 --> 00:07:51,520
Two days later Hitler postponed
Operation Sea Lion indefinitely.

124
00:07:51,570 --> 00:07:55,330
British bombers had destroyed many
of the barges gathered in the ports,

125
00:07:55,350 --> 00:07:57,880
and the autumn storms were coming.

126
00:07:57,990 --> 00:08:02,580
In October the landing was
put off until spring 1941.

127
00:08:02,750 --> 00:08:05,740
After that it was never
seriously considered again.

128
00:08:07,290 --> 00:08:12,390
The Luftwaffe now attacked mainly at
night, targeting cities and industry.

129
00:08:12,410 --> 00:08:16,150
On the night of 14 to 15
November it was Coventry's turn.

130
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:20,650
About 500 bombers reduced the city
centre and the cathedral to rubble,

131
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:22,650
and more than 500 people died.

132
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:26,620
German propaganda then coined
the word "coventrieren".

133
00:08:26,670 --> 00:08:30,110
On 29 December the City of London burned.

134
00:08:30,130 --> 00:08:32,550
A photo of the dome of St Paul's Cathedral

135
00:08:32,570 --> 00:08:35,840
amid smoke and flames
went around the world.

136
00:08:36,090 --> 00:08:39,140
The night raids lasted until May 1941.

137
00:08:39,630 --> 00:08:44,530
By then more than 40,000 civilians
had been killed in Britain.

138
00:08:44,550 --> 00:08:47,180
But the morale of the
population did not break,

139
00:08:47,270 --> 00:08:49,620
and arms production continued.

140
00:08:51,170 --> 00:08:54,830
Today the Battle of Britain is
regarded as the first major battle

141
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,110
to be decided in the air alone.

142
00:08:57,130 --> 00:09:02,870
By the end of October 1940 the
Luftwaffe had lost about 1,900 aircraft,

143
00:09:02,890 --> 00:09:05,700
Fighter Command about 1,000 fighters.

144
00:09:06,210 --> 00:09:11,030
544 pilots and aircrew of
Fighter Command were killed,

145
00:09:11,050 --> 00:09:15,070
on the German side more than 2,500 airmen.

146
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:17,750
The Luftwaffe had
underestimated its opponent,

147
00:09:17,770 --> 00:09:22,060
changed its targets several times
and armed its bombers too lightly.

148
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:23,850
Britain stayed in the war

149
00:09:23,870 --> 00:09:28,270
and later became the springboard
for the Allied landing in France.

150
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:30,810
Hitler, however, turned to another target.

151
00:09:30,830 --> 00:09:36,290
In summer 1940 he had already ordered
plans for a war against the Soviet Union,

152
00:09:36,830 --> 00:09:39,620
and in December he signed
the directive for it.
